A selector decides which elements a rule touches. Getting it wrong is the number one reason a stylesheet seems to do nothing.

## The Main Selectors

- Element: `p` styles every paragraph.
- Class: `.form-field` styles anything with `class="form-field"`.
- ID: `#submit` styles the single element with that id.
- Descendant: `.form-grid input` styles inputs inside the grid.
- Group: `h1, h2` applies one rule to both.

```css
.form-field label {
  font-weight: 600;
}
#submit {
  background: #2563eb;
}
```

## Pseudo Classes

Pseudo classes describe a state rather than a tag name.

```css
.btn:hover {
  background: #1d4ed8;
}
.btn:focus {
  outline: 3px solid #93c5fd;
}
```

`:hover` reacts to the pointer, `:focus` reacts to keyboard and click focus, and `:active` is the pressed moment. A visible `:focus` style is not optional, because keyboard users navigate your form with the tab key.

## Specificity Rules

1. Inline styles beat everything else.
2. IDs beat classes, and classes beat element selectors.
3. When two rules tie, the last one in the stylesheet wins.

Keep selectors short. Three chained classes usually means the markup needs a rethink instead.

Every element is a box made of four layers. Once you can picture them, padding versus margin stops being a guessing game.

## The Four Layers

- **Content**: the text, input, or image itself.
- **Padding**: space inside the border, around the content.
- **Border**: the edge line around the padding.
- **Margin**: space outside the box, between it and its neighbours.

## Why border-box Wins

```css
* {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
.card {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
}
```

With the default `content-box`, that card is 100 percent wide **plus** 40 pixels of padding plus borders, so it overflows its container. With `border-box`, width includes padding and border, and the card stays put. This single line prevents a whole family of layout bugs.

## Spacing in Practice

```css
.form-field {
  margin-bottom: 20px;
  padding: 12px;
}
```

Use margin to separate siblings and padding to give content breathing room inside its own box.

## Common Bugs

- A child with `width: 100%` and horizontal padding overflows without `border-box`.
- Adjacent vertical margins collapse to the larger value instead of adding up.
- Inline elements ignore top and bottom margin; wrap them in a block element.
- DevTools shows the box model diagram for the node you select, which is the fastest way to find an unexpected 8 pixel gap.

With selectors and the box model in place, styling becomes a set of repeatable choices: colour, type, spacing, and one central place to change them.

## Connecting CSS to HTML

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css" />
```

A linked stylesheet is the right default for every page. Inline styles and `<style>` blocks are for one-off exceptions only, because scattered rules are hard to find later.

## CSS Variables as a Design System

```css
:root {
  --primary: #2563eb;
  --primary-hover: #1d4ed8;
}
.btn-primary {
  background: var(--primary);
  color: #fff;
}
.btn-primary:hover {
  background: var(--primary-hover);
}
```

Define each value once in `:root` and reference it with `var()`. Changing the theme later becomes one edit instead of ten, exactly like the `style.css` you styled the form with this week.

## Layout and Responsive Basics

- `display: flex` with `justify-content: center` centres a card horizontally.
- `grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr` gives two equal columns for a form.
- A media query collapses that grid on phones:

```css
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  .form-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
```

## Practical Tips

- Give every interactive element `:hover`, `:focus`, and `:active` states.
- Reset default margins with a small `*` rule, but keep `box-sizing: border-box`.
- Style in order: structure, fields, error states, then buttons.

Pseudo-elements let you inject decorative content before or after an element without adding a single tag to the HTML.

## The Two You Will Use

```css
h2::before {
  content: "\2192 ";
  color: #2563eb;
}
h2::after {
  content: "";
  display: block;
  width: 40px;
  height: 3px;
  background: #2563eb;
}
```

Both require a `content` property, even when it is empty. Without `content`, nothing renders at all.

## Decorations From the Class Demos

- Custom bullets: `li::before` with `content: "Fries icon"` and `list-style: none`.
- Tooltips: `content: attr(data-tip)` on hover, faded in with an `opacity` transition.
- Badges: `content: "NEW"` positioned absolutely in a corner.
- Numbered steps: `counter-reset` on the list and `content: counter(step)` on each item.

## An Underline That Grows on Hover

```css
h2 {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}
h2::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  bottom: -4px;
  width: 0;
  height: 3px;
  background: #2563eb;
  transition: width 0.3s ease;
}
h2:hover::after {
  width: 100%;
}
```

## Limits

`::before` and `::after` are not real DOM nodes: JavaScript cannot `querySelector` them and screen readers may skip their text. Keep important information in the HTML and use these for decoration only.

Flexbox is a one dimensional layout system. Items line up along a single main axis, and a perpendicular cross axis, and you control the two axes separately.

## Container Controls

```css
.navbar {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
  align-items: center;
}
```

- `justify-content` distributes space along the main axis: `flex-start`, `center`, `end`, `space-between`, `space-around`.
- `align-items` aligns on the cross axis: `stretch`, `center`, `flex-start`.
- `flex-wrap: wrap` lets items move to a new line instead of shrinking forever.

## The Rule That Centres Anything

```css
.center {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

The container needs a height, otherwise centring has no space to work in. This is the classic flexbox trick from the class demos.

## Responsive Cards Without Media Queries

```css
.card-row {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 16px;
}
.card-row .card {
  flex: 1 1 200px;
}
```

`flex` is grow, shrink, basis. A 200px basis means the cards wrap naturally as the window narrows, which is how the record shelf in `extra/crate-digging` works.

## Watch the Axis Swap

When you set `flex-direction: column`, `justify-content` controls vertical spacing and `align-items` controls horizontal. Forgetting that swap is the classic flexbox bug.

Grid is two dimensional: you decide rows and columns at the same time, which is why whole page layouts become short and readable.

## Defining Tracks

```css
.gallery {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(140px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}
```

`gap` replaces margin hacks, `fr` means a share of the free space, and `auto-fit` adds or removes columns as you resize, so no media query is needed at all.

## Named Areas

```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 200px;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header header header"
    "nav    main   aside"
    "footer footer footer";
  gap: 12px;
}
.page header { grid-area: header; }
.page main   { grid-area: main; }
```

Rearranging the whole page then becomes one edit to the template string, which is exactly what `extra/the-kigali-review` does with its editorial layout.

## Spanning

- `grid-column: span 2` makes a featured card two columns wide.
- `grid-row: span 2` stretches it down as well.
- Each named area must be rectangular or the template will not parse.

## Grid or Flexbox

- Rows and columns together, or a page skeleton: Grid.
- A single row of items that must centre or space evenly: Flexbox.
- They compose: a grid cell can itself be a flex container.

Two tools create motion. A `transition` smooths a change between two states, and `@keyframes` scripts a custom movement over time.

## Transitions

```css
.button {
  background: #2563eb;
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease, transform 0.3s ease;
}
.button:hover {
  background: #1d4ed8;
  transform: scale(1.05);
}
```

The shorthand reads property, duration, timing function, delay. Only properties you change on the target state animate, so a hover rule with no changed property does nothing.

## Keyframe Animations

```css
@keyframes bounce {
  0%, 100% { transform: translateY(0); }
  50%      { transform: translateY(-20px); }
}
.ball {
  animation: bounce 0.8s ease-in-out infinite;
}
```

Percentages are stops along the timeline. Class demos also include a slide in, a pulsing dot, a spinner, and a shake on error.

## Performance Rules

- Animate `transform` and `opacity`: they are composited and stay smooth at 60fps.
- Avoid animating `width`, `height`, `top`, and `left` on large elements, because each change forces layout.
- Prefer `transition` for hover states and `animation` for anything that runs on load or loops.

## Respect Reduced Motion

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .pulse, .bounce, .spinner { animation: none; }
  .btn-transition { transition: none; }
}
```

Some visitors feel unwell from moving content. Switch infinite loops off completely rather than speeding them up, because a faster infinite bounce is still an infinite bounce.

Objects group related data and behaviour. Nearly every real dataset in JavaScript is an object, or an array of objects.

## Creating and Accessing

```js
const student = { name: "David", cohort: "C2" };
student.name;      // dot access
student["cohort"]; // bracket access
student.city = "Kigali"; // add a property
delete student.city;     // remove a property
```

Brackets are required for keys with spaces, or when the key lives in a variable: `student[key]`.

## Methods and this

```js
const counter = {
  total: 0,
  add(n) {
    this.total += n;
    return this;
  },
};
counter.add(5).add(3); // chained, total is 8
```

A method's `this` is the object that called it. An arrow function has no `this` of its own, so never write object methods or constructors as arrows.

## Destructuring, Spread, and Nesting

```js
const { name, cohort = "C1" } = student;
const merged = { ...defaults, ...overrides };
university.location?.coordinates; // optional chaining
Object.entries({ a: 1 });         // [["a", 1]]
```

- Destructure in function parameters for clean signatures such as `printUser({ name, email })`.
- Spread copies shallowly, so nested objects are still shared.
- `Object.keys`, `Object.values`, and `Object.entries` make looping over data simple.

Classes are templates for objects that share structure and behaviour. They organise bigger programs so related data and functions live together instead of drifting apart.

## Class and Constructor

```js
class Student {
  constructor(name, cohort) {
    this.name = name;
    this.cohort = cohort;
  }
  introduce() {
    return "Hi, I am " + this.name;
  }
}
const d = new Student("David", "C2");
```

`new` creates the object and calls `constructor` for you. Methods are shared on the prototype, so every instance reuses one copy.

## Getters, Setters, and Static

```js
class Rectangle {
  constructor(w, h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }
  get area() { return this.w * this.h; }
  static units() { return "metres"; }
}
Rectangle.units(); // called on the class, not on an instance
```

A getter runs like a property (`rect.area`), a setter intercepts assignment, and `static` members belong to the class itself, which is how `MathHelper.add()` style helpers work.

## Inheritance and Privacy

```js
class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
  constructor(owner) {
    super(owner); // must run before this
    this.rate = 0.05;
  }
}
```

- `extends` links the child to the parent, and `super()` must be called before you use `this`.
- Override a method by redefining it, and call `super.method()` to reuse the parent version.
- Private fields use `#balance` and truly hide data, unlike a leading underscore, which is only a naming convention.

Once you can select elements, the next step is reading and changing them. jQuery groups this into a small set of memorable methods.

## Content and Values

```js
$("#preview-title").text("Plain text");
$("#preview-copy").html("Text with <strong>HTML</strong>");
const email = $("#email").val();
```

`.text()` is the safe default. Use `.html()` only when you intend to insert markup, and `.val()` for form fields, because inputs have no text node for you to read.

## Attributes, Classes, and CSS

```js
$(".preview-card").attr("data-state", "updated");
$(".preview-card").addClass("is-highlighted");
$(".preview-card").toggleClass("is-compact");
$("#preview-title").css("color", "#d94841");
```

Prefer classes for reusable visual states. One class change keeps the styling in your stylesheet instead of scattered across your JavaScript.

## Adding and Removing Nodes

```js
const item = $("<li>", { text: "New item", class: "new-item" });
$("#created-list").append(item);
$("#created-list").children("li").last().remove();
```

- `.append()` and `.prepend()` add content inside the parent.
- `.before()` and `.after()` insert around the selection.
- `.remove()` deletes the element, `.empty()` clears its children, `.clone()` copies it.

Events connect the page to the user: clicks, typing, submits. In jQuery one method, `.on()`, covers them all.

## Wiring a Handler

```js
$("#count-button").on("click", function () {
  $("#click-output").text("The button was clicked.");
});
```

Common events are `click`, `change`, `input`, `submit`, `keydown`, `mouseenter`, and `mouseleave`. Attach the handler after the element exists, or jQuery will find nothing to bind to.

## The Event Object

```js
$("form").on("submit", function (event) {
  event.preventDefault(); // stop the browser submitting
  console.log(event.type); // "submit"
});
```

`preventDefault()` is how you take over a form so your own validation runs first. Inside a normal function, `this` is the element that received the event, and you can wrap it with `$(this)` to call jQuery methods on it.

## Event Delegation

```js
$("#button-rack").on("click", ".dynamic-button", function () {
  $("#output").text($(this).data("message"));
});
```

The handler sits on a parent and fires only for matching descendants, so buttons created after page load still work. Use delegation whenever the child elements are added dynamically, and check `event.which` when you need a key or button number.

Two tools cover most scraping needs. Cheerio parses static HTML with familiar CSS selectors, and Playwright drives a real browser for content that JavaScript creates.

## Cheerio for Static Pages

```js
const cheerio = require("cheerio");
const $ = cheerio.load(html);
$(".post").length;
$(".post-title").text().trim();
$(".post-link").attr("href");
```

Install it inside the activity folder with `npm install`, then run `npm run scrape`. If the data is already in the initial HTML and nothing needs clicking, Cheerio is enough and much faster.

## Playwright for Dynamic Pages

```js
const browser = await chromium.launch({ headless: true });
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.goto(url);
await page.locator(".post").first().waitFor();
await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Load more reports" }).click();
```

Playwright waits for content, clicks, fills forms, and takes screenshots. Setup is heavier because it downloads a browser with `npx playwright install chromium`.

## Choosing a Tool

| Situation | Tool |
| --- | --- |
| Data already in the HTML | Cheerio |
| Load more buttons or scrolling | Playwright |
| JSON or XML from an API | fetch, not a scraper |
| Structured data in the page | read the JSON-LD first |

## Rule of Thumb

Use the simplest tool that does the job, and prefer an official API whenever one exists. Browser automation is powerful, but slower and more expensive than parsing HTML you already have.
